protesters took to the streets of Hong  Kong beginning in April 20 1940 after  the Hong Kong government proposed a bill  permitting extradition to China since  then Hong Kong chief executive Carrie  Lam a position appointed by Beijing has  pledged to withdraw the bill but the  anti-government protests have evolved  past this issue and protesters are now  demanding more democratic freedoms in  Hong Kong honestly it's hard to see how  this will all end even if Carrie Lam  gets the boot that's probably not enough  to satisfy protesters and it seems  equally unlikely that Beijing will yield  to their demands the Chinese Communist  Party does not want to encourage similar  behavior and other regions problematic  to the regime the CCP also wants to show  Taiwan that the one country two systems'  formula works so far China has avoided a  Tiananmen style crackdown opting instead  for repressive police tactics the  Chinese President Xi Jinping has also  warned that anyone attempting to split  China would be crushed how the Communist  Party decides to ultimately address the  protests may change the course of  China's relationship with Hong Kong  forever which side do you think will  ultimately get its way let us know in  our poll also let us know what other  topics you'd like our scholars to cover  in 60 seconds and be sure to LIKE and  subscribe for more research and videos  from AEI
 

